"[2604.15602] GroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.15602)\nciteturn41349view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.15602\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Apr 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 1 Sep 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:GroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jixuan Leng , cite7†Si Si , cite8†Hsiang-Fu Yu , cite9†Vinod Raman , cite10†Inderjit S. Dhillon L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled GroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization, by Jixuan Leng and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Preference optimization is widely used to align Large Language Models (LLMs) with preference feedback. However, most existing methods train on a single positive-negative pair per prompt, discarding additional supervision available in preference datasets that typically contain multiple candidate responses.\nL17: Motivated by this limitation, recent work explores group-wise preference optimization, which jointly contrasts multiple responses for the same prompt, but its empirical behavior and scalability remain underexplored due to the memory overhead of group-coupled objectives. In this work, we present a unified empirical and systems study of group-wise preference optimization and develop a memory-efficient implementation for group-coupled objectives.\nL18: By instantiating first-order linearization with objective-specific per-response coefficients, our implementation preserves first-order gradients while decoupling samples during backpropagation, substantially reducing peak memory usage and enabling scalable training with larger groups. Across offline and online settings, we show that leveraging multiple responses consistently outperforms single-pair training.\nL19: Furthermore, incorporating a negative log-likelihood (NLL) term on positive responses is critical for both performance gains and training stability.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2604.15602 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2604.15602v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.15602†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jixuan Leng [cite16†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Fri, 17 Apr 2026 00:56:59 UTC (3,825 KB)\nL28: [v2] Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:34:39 UTC (4,447 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled GroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization, by Jixuan Leng and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-04 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and C