ion is Broken L117: \nL118: Shiv Sundram, Akhilesh Balasingam, Thierry Tambe, Kunle Olukotun, and Fredrik Kjolstad (Stanford University)\nL119: ### Session 7\nL120: \nL121:   * cite46†From Pragmas to Partners: A Symbiotic Evolution of Agentic High-Level Synthesis L122: \nL123: Niansong Zhang and Sunwoo Kim (Cornell University); Shreesha Srinath (Cerebras Systems); Zhiru Zhang (Cornell University)\nL124: \nL125:   * cite47†A Position on Tropical-Time for HLS virtual\nL126: \nL127: Sijie Kong (UC Santa Barbara); Jonathan Balkind (UCSB)\nL128: \nL129:   * cite48†Unifying HLS and RTL with Timeline Types --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Black Box to Toolbox: Human-Centered Explainability in Developer-Authored Agent Rules | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/19700278)\nciteturn41349view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41348view2\",\"pattern\":\"Published\"}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Image L1: \nL2: cite1†HCXAI 2026 — Human-Centered Explainable AI Workshop L3: \nL4: Published April 13, 2026 | Version v1\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Conference paper Open\nL7: \nL8: # From Black Box to Toolbox: Human-Centered Explainability in Developer-Authored Agent Rules\nL9: \nL10: ### Authors/Creators\nL11: \nL12:   * cite3†Romero, Lilia Pérez L13:   * cite4†Dong, Tao L14:   * cite5†Ferrari-Church, Ben L15: ## Description\nL16: As AI agents permeate software development, developers are embracing markdown-based rule files (e.g., AGENTS.md) to provide the organizational context that general-purpose models often lack. This emerging practice, nonetheless, is undermined by a critical explainability challenge. Through a qualitative study of 12 professional software developers who author agent rules for their respective projects, we found that developers lack a reliable way to verify whether an agent consistently follow specific rules.\nL17: Further more, they struggle to attribute observable agent behaviors to individual instructions in agent rule files. Instead, they often rely on subjective \"vibe checks\" to test these rules. Our findings also highlight a lack of centralized governance of agent rules for common libraries, leading to redundant work and the erosion of a shared understanding about agent behavior.\nL18: We argue for a transition toward rigorous rule traceability and library-level governance to transform agent customization from an ad-hoc craft into an evidence-based practice.\nL19: ## Notes\nL20: \nL21: Proceedings of the CHI 2026 Workshop on Human-Centered Explainable AI (HCXAI); April 13–17, 2026; Barcelona, Spain.\nL22: \nL23: ## Files\nL24: \nL25: ###\nL26: \nL27: HCXAI2026_paper_14.pdf\nL28: \nL29: cite6†Preview L30: ###\nL31: \nL32: Files (546.2 kB)\nL33: \nL34: Name  | Size  |\nL35: --- | --- | ---\nL36: cite7†HCXAI2026_paper_14.pdf md5:a9908771647b73814e598403dfb6a10d  | 546.2 kB  | cite6†Preview cite7†Download L37: \nL38: 234\nL39: \nL40: Views\nL41: \nL42: 199\nL43: \nL44: Downloads\nL45: \nL46: Show more details\nL47: \nL48:  | All versions  | This version\nL49: --- | --- | ---\nL50: Views  | 234  | 234\nL51: Downloads  | 199  | 199\nL52: Data volume  | 115.2 MB  | 115.2 MB\nL53: \nL54: cite8†More info on how stats are collected.... L55: \nL56: ## Versions\nL57: \nL58: ## External resources\nL59: \nL60: Indexed in\nL61: \nL62:   * cite9†Image L63: \nL64: cite10†OpenAIRE†explore.openaire.eu L65: \nL66: ## Communities\nL67: ## Keywords and subjects\nL68: \nL69:   * cite11†Human-Centered AI L70:   * cite12†Explainable AI (XAI) L71:   * cite13†Software Engineering L72:   * cite14†AI Agents L73: \nL74: ## Details\nL75: \nL76: DOI\nL77:     cite15†Image: Get the DOI badge! L78: \nL79: DOI Badge\nL80: \nL81: #### DOI\nL82: \nL83: ####\nL84: \nL85:     10.5281/zenodo.19700278\nL86: \nL87: ### Markdown\nL88: \nL89:     [![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo